[38;2;124;124;124mWe have $G$ a countable discrete group, $l^\infty(G)$ the bounded functions on [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m$G$, and the left regular action: $(s \cdot f)(g) = f(s^{-1}g)$. We view [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m$l^\infty(G) \subseteq B(l^2(G))$ as multiplication operators (diagonal [0m
[38;2;124;124;124moperators in the basis $\{\delta_g : g \in G\}$).[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mThe question: for every $s \in G$, does there exist $u_s \in B(l^2(G))$ such [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mthat $s \cdot f = u_s f u_s^*$ for all $f \in l^\infty(G)$?[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mThe answer is YES. The unitary is the left regular representation $\lambda_s$.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mLet me recall: The left regular representation $\lambda_s \in B(l^2(G))$ is [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mdefined by $(\lambda_s \xi)(g) = \xi(s^{-1}g)$ for $\xi \in l^2(G)$.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mNow, viewing $f \in l^\infty(G)$ as a multiplication operator $M_f$ on $l^2(G)$:[0m[38;2;124;124;124m$(M_f \xi)(g) = f(g) \xi(g)$.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mWe need to check: $\lambda_s M_f \lambda_s^* = M_{s \cdot f}$.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mCompute: $(\lambda_s M_f \lambda_s^* \xi)(g) = (M_f \lambda_s^* \xi)(s^{-1}g) = [0m[38;2;124;124;124mf(s^{-1}g) (\lambda_s^* \xi)(s^{-1}g)$.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mNow $\lambda_s^* = \lambda_{s^{-1}}$, so $(\lambda_s^* \xi)(h) = \xi(s h)$. Thus[0m[38;2;124;124;124m$(\lambda_s^* \xi)(s^{-1}g) = \xi(s \cdot s^{-1} g) = \xi(g)$.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mSo $(\lambda_s M_f \lambda_s^* \xi)(g) = f(s^{-1}g) \xi(g) = (s \cdot f)(g) [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m\xi(g) = (M_{s \cdot f} \xi)(g)$.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mTherefore $\lambda_s M_f \lambda_s^* = M_{s \cdot f}$, which means $s \cdot f = [0m[38;2;124;124;124m\lambda_s f \lambda_s^*$.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mSo yes, the answer is true, and $u_s = \lambda_s$ (the left regular [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mrepresentation) works. Moreover $\lambda_s$ is a unitary.[0m

